INT. RADIO STATION BROADCAST BOOTH - NIGHT

A glass-walled booth lined with egg-crate foam. Dials, faders, a microphone on a swan neck. Beyond the glass, a dim studio and a bank of dead windows. A red ON AIR sign glows above the door.

Six people sit around a small round table in the booth, hands flat on the wood. A single candle burns in the center.

OSWALD FERRIS-MBEKI (60s, station manager, cardigan, pipe unlit) presides. LAVINIA CRUZ-HOLLANDER (40s, medium, shawl, silver rings on every finger) closes her eyes. DEREK PUTNAM-ADEBAYO (30s, sound engineer, headphones round his neck) rests a hand on the mixing board. MARGUERITE LACROIX (70s, retired host, silk scarf, oxygen tank) watches the flame. HAZEL OKONKWO-DREW (20s, intern, notebook) writes furiously. NIGEL STRAND (50s, owner's lawyer, damp raincoat) stares at the exit.

LAVINIA
(eyes closed)
We are here to hear what was never aired.

OSWALD
Hush, everyone.

NIGEL
This is absurd.

MARGUERITE
Let her work, dear.

The candle flame bows. Derek glances at the board. Every needle is dead.

DEREK
Nothing's powered.

HAZEL
(whispering)
That's what I wrote.

LAVINIA
He is close. A man with a heavy voice. He says...

A crackle on the monitor speaker.

VOICE (V.O.)
(distorted, from the speaker)
...ten minutes to air.

Oswald drops his pipe. Marguerite covers her mouth. Derek leans toward the board. Nigel pushes his chair back.

NIGEL
That's a recording. Someone's playing a tape.

DEREK
There's no tape. Nothing's plugged in.

VOICE (V.O.)
(from the speaker)
...cut the lights, Marguerite.

Marguerite is staring at the speaker. Her hand shakes. The oxygen tank hisses.

MARGUERITE
That is Vincent.

OSWALD
Vincent is dead, Marguerite.

MARGUERITE
Thirty years.

VOICE (V.O.)
(from the speaker)
...tell them what you did.

Lavinia's eyes fly open. She is not looking at the speaker. She is looking at Nigel.

LAVINIA
It's you.

NIGEL
I beg your pardon?

LAVINIA
He is saying your name.

NIGEL
He is not.

VOICE (V.O.)
(from the speaker)
...Nigel.

Nigel slams a palm onto the table. The candle gutters. The ON AIR sign flashes. Hazel scribbles. Derek lunges for the board. Oswald stands, knocking over his chair. The whole booth goes black.

In the dark, a single click. A match is lit. Marguerite's face floats above the flame, calm, steady, ancient.

MARGUERITE
Sit down, Mr. Strand.

NIGEL
(whispering)
Please.

MARGUERITE
Vincent kept very good notes.

She holds up a small cassette. The match burns her finger. She doesn't flinch. In the glow, five faces stare at the sixth, who is no longer looking at the exit at all.
